            {
              "id": "eb2cd348-d530-3f57-93ab-407dc81c48a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur in the C function `strlen()`. This function is used to determine the length of a string, but it does not perform any bounds checking. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading until it encounters a null byte, potentially leading to a buffer overflow. This can result in undefined behavior, including program crashes, data corruption, and even code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `sprintf()` is used to append a formatted string to the end of an existing string. However, if the size of the formatted string exceeds the remaining space in the destination buffer, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo prevent buffer overflow vulnerabilities, it is important to always perform bounds checking before writing to a buffer. This can be done by ensuring that the size of the data being written does not exceed the size of the buffer.\n\nIn addition, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()` and `sprintf()`, such as `strnlen()` and `snprintf()`, which take an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of bytes to read or write.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t name_len = strnlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name, MAX_NAME_LEN);\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name + name_len, MAX_NAME_LEN - name_len, \"[%\" PRId64 \"]\", y);\n```\n\nIn this fix, `strnlen()` is used to safely determine the length of the string, and `snprintf()` is used to prevent writing beyond the end of the buffer. `MAX_NAME_LEN` should be defined as the size of the `name` buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `sprintf()`\n- `string.h` for `strlen()`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b517e81f-579c-3f15-bf99-3de0628b5e03",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name (`fname`) is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, consider using a function that allows for more secure handling of files. Always validate and sanitize input from untrusted sources. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream infile;\ninfile.open(fname, std::ios::binary);\n```\n\nIn this example, the C++ `ifstream` class is used to open the file in binary mode. This class provides more secure file handling than the C `fopen` function.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f4636ae9-3aeb-37f9-a8f2-67b5480d36a9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur when using the `strlen` function in C++. This function does not check for the end of the string, and if the string is not null-terminated, it can lead to a buffer overflow. This can cause the program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `sprintf` function is used to append a string to `(*kv)[*kvnum].name`. If the length of the resulting string exceeds the size of the buffer, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that your strings are null-terminated and that you do not exceed the size of your buffers. You should also consider using safer alternatives to `sprintf`, such as `snprintf`, which allows you to specify the maximum number of characters to be written to the buffer, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code snippet using `snprintf`:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name + strlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name), sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name) - strlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name), \".instance[%\" PRId64 \"]\", cur_instance);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following libraries:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `sprintf` and `snprintf`\n- `string.h` for `strlen`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "24b32dbb-ee86-35a5-8821-37a76386e4ca",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name (`fname`) is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, consider using a function that allows for more secure handling of files. Always validate and sanitize input from untrusted sources. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream infile;\ninfile.open(fname, std::ios::binary);\n```\n\nIn this example, the C++ `ifstream` class is used to open the file in binary mode. This class provides more secure file handling than the C `fopen` function.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fb30d248-8c61-3853-ad65-ad3708750aae",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can result in crashes, incorrect behavior, or potential execution of malicious code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(info.container_name) > 0`, there is a potential risk if `info.container_name` is not a null-terminated string. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter to specify the maximum length of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strlen()` function with `strnlen()`. The second parameter to `strnlen()` should be the maximum size of `info.container_name`. \n\n```cpp\nstrnlen(info.container_name, sizeof(info.container_name)) > 0\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed for the original code. The `strnlen()` function is also part of the C standard library, but it is not available in all environments. If `strnlen()` is not available, you may need to implement a similar function yourself.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7a0fbe1c-3fe0-3309-b9b3-151c1cb05f8e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strcpy` and instead use safer string handling functions that include buffer size as a parameter, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of bytes is written to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(info.container_name, container_id.c_str(), sizeof(info.container_name) - 1);\ninfo.container_name[sizeof(info.container_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `container_id` to `info.container_name`, but it will not write more than `sizeof(info.container_name) - 1` bytes. The last byte is set to the null character to ensure that `info.container_name` is a null-terminated string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strncpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "698121a6-d90d-3bac-a372-926809dbf2c7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(container_type_name[i])`, the vulnerability arises if `container_type_name[i]` is not a null-terminated string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen()`. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to read.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `container_type_name` is an array of strings, you can ensure null-termination as follows:\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure null-termination\ncontainer_type_name[i][sizeof(container_type_name[i]) - 1] = '\\0';\n\n// Now it's safe to call strlen\nsize_t len = strlen(container_type_name[i]);\n```\n\nIf you have control over the creation of the strings, ensure they are null-terminated at creation time.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5ae42019-828f-382e-8ef2-c543c110ffc1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a 'UB Risk' (undefined behavior risk) due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis indicates that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can lead to undefined behavior when using functions like memcpy. Additionally, the source may be null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the destination is not a pointer-based member, making it less likely to be null, but the source's safety cannot be guaranteed. The verdict is 'GENUINE', confirming this is a real and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(&pmmetrics_version, &buf1[12], 4);\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are valid and not null before performing the operation. If the source or destination may be null, add explicit checks to avoid dereferencing null pointers.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "06b1e788-5be4-3467-aaae-0a2c320ca462",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the potential for null pointer dereference, and the verdict is marked as genuine. The code copies a string into a destination buffer using a function that does not check if the destination pointer is null. The analysis indicates that the destination pointer may be null at this point, and there is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this. The source is a C++ string's c_str(), which is generally safe, but the lack of a null check on the destination is a significant risk. There is also no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to further issues if the copied string is exactly the buffer size. The absence of a guard, the lack of null-termination, and the possibility of a null destination pointer all contribute to the risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the destination buffer, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add an explicit check for the destination buffer before the copy operation. Also, ensure that the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy to prevent string-related vulnerabilities.\n\n```cpp\nif (info.name != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(info.name, name.c_str(), std::min((unsigned long)AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, name.length()));\n    info.name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nIf the destination buffer is a fixed-size array, ensure that the size used in the copy does not exceed the buffer's capacity, and always null-terminate the string. If the buffer can be null, handle the error appropriately (e.g., log an error, return an error code, or throw an exception).\n\n## References\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1e8a628d-b4db-3245-9bb3-f031f781c813",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are related by data flow, which means they may refer to overlapping memory areas. This can cause undefined behavior with functions like memcpy. Additionally, the source may be null at this call site, which further increases the risk of a crash or security issue. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the length argument is not derived from the destination's capacity, making it more likely that this is a real problem.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `std::memmove` instead of `std::memcpy`. `std::memmove` is designed to safely handle overlapping source and destination regions.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(&cper_data[data_idx], reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header), header->record_length);\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that `header` is not null and that `header->record_length` does not exceed the bounds of the destination buffer. Add explicit checks before the copy operation:\n\n```cpp\nif (header != nullptr && header->record_length <= sizeof(cper_data) - data_idx) {\n    std::memmove(&cper_data[data_idx], reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header), header->record_length);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "51972bad-11a3-33a2-a9c7-7b7177282a69",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the absence of null pointer checks for either the source or destination buffer. The code copies a string from a C++ string object to a destination buffer using a fixed maximum length minus one, but there is no evidence of a check to ensure that the destination pointer is not null before the copy operation. The source is a C++ string's c_str(), which is generally safe, but the destination may be null, as there is no guard or validation present. This could lead to a crash or other undefined behavior if the destination pointer is ever null at runtime. The probability of this being a false positive is very low, as the analysis specifically points out the lack of null checks for the destination pointer, which is a common source of critical bugs in C/C++ code.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the destination buffer, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. This prevents undefined behavior and potential crashes. For example, add a check before the copy operation:\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_info && cpu_info->model_name) {\n    strncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    cpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the destination buffer is valid before performing the copy. Additionally, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after strncpy to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "452bbef9-a323-3333-857f-2e7406dd6bdd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, instead of using `fopen`, you can use the `fstream` class from the C++ Standard Library which provides a higher level of abstraction and more control over file operations.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `fstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string path_max_link_speed;\n// ... (initialize path_max_link_speed)\nstd::ifstream file(path_max_link_speed);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for file operations\n- `<string>`: for string operations\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a85c9998-02cd-36ca-aeb9-d947e77f3af9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is manipulated or controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, consider using `fstream` objects from the C++ Standard Library, which provide a higher level of abstraction and are generally safer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` object from the C++ Standard Library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\nstd::ifstream file(path_max_link_width.c_str());\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for the `ifstream` object\n- `<string>`: for the `string` object and its `c_str()` method\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e2e60ecd-2528-39f3-a361-6e72834934a6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path (`sysfs_path`) is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fopen` such as `fopen_s` or `fstream`. These functions provide better error handling and are less prone to vulnerabilities. Additionally, always validate and sanitize input that will be used as a file path to prevent path traversal or other types of attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with `fstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(sysfs_path.c_str(), std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "37cdc37c-048b-35e7-9d93-b1aa2ebfb859",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior with functions like memcpy. The verdict for this call site is also marked as genuine. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and neither the source nor the destination is null at this point, which means the main risk is the potential for overlapping memory regions. The destination is a pointer type, and the size calculation is not directly tied to a known buffer capacity, increasing the risk. Developers should ensure that the source and destination do not overlap or use a function that safely handles overlapping regions.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the use of `std::memcpy` with `std::memmove`, which is designed to safely handle overlapping regions. This change ensures that the copy operation is well-defined even if the memory areas overlap.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(cpu_set, bitmask.data(), cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a7cece13-1c56-3fdf-a0c9-f78ec0f12833",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are related by data flow, indicating that they may refer to overlapping memory areas. This can cause undefined behavior when using functions like memcpy, which do not support overlapping regions. The risk is further supported by the explicit classification and verdict. There are no guards or checks in place to prevent this overlap, and the destination and source are both confirmed to be non-null, so the main concern is the potential for memory overlap.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the use of `std::memcpy` with `std::memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. This change ensures that the copy operation will not result in data corruption or crashes if the regions overlap.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size);\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
