            {
              "id": "7e930184-6628-3338-94bb-f376c1bdacff",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are known to be insecure. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. \n\nIn this specific case, the function `std::getenv(name)` is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in an environment block. The problem is that this block can be modified by another process, leading to potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv(name)` in your code. Instead, consider using more secure alternatives that do not have the same vulnerabilities. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `std::getenv(name)`, you can use the `std::getenv_s` function, which is a more secure alternative. Here is an example of how to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nsize_t requiredSize;\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, name);\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"The environment variable doesn't exist.\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\nchar* value = (char*)malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\nif (!value)\n{\n    printf(\"Failed to allocate memory.\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, name);\nprintf(\"%s\\n\", value);\nfree(value);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<stdlib.h>`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2fd20e28-e306-3773-9596-5e8a08501aaf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The dataflow analysis indicates that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source is not null, which could also lead to a crash. The destination is guarded as non-null, but the source is not. The use of a macro for the copy length does not guarantee safety, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy. These factors all contribute to the high confidence in this being a real issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the string copy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as `memmove`, but only if the data is not null-terminated or if you handle null-termination explicitly. Additionally, always check that the source pointer is not null before copying, and ensure the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after the copy to prevent string-related vulnerabilities.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```c\nif (vbios_version != NULL && info != NULL && info->version != vbios_version) {\n    strncpy(info->version, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    info->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n- Check that the source is not null.\n- Ensure the source and destination do not overlap.\n- Copy up to one less than the buffer size and explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6420b844-b1a1-3469-bf3d-8f41319de2d7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The dataflow analysis suggests that the destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, the source pointer may be null, which could also lead to a crash or further undefined behavior. There is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the copy, increasing the risk of string-related errors. The destination is confirmed to be non-null before the call, but the source's validity cannot be guaranteed. These factors indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to ensure program correctness and security.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the string copy operation, first ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. Additionally, always ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated after the copy to prevent string-related errors.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure source and destination do not overlap, or use memmove if overlap is possible\nmemmove(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n// Explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer\ninfo->part_number[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf you are certain that the source and destination never overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and consider adding runtime assertions to enforce it during development.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
