            {
              "id": "13437bfe-bd00-3bcd-b8e7-a062cdad5011",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination buffers in the strncpy call. The dataflow analysis indicates that the destination and source may overlap, which is not allowed by the strncpy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, the source pointer may be null at this call site, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. There is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the call, and there are no guards or checks to ensure the safety of the operation. The destination buffer is not confirmed to be large enough, and the count argument is not derived from the destination's capacity. All these factors contribute to a high confidence that this is a real and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the strncpy call, first ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use a function that safely handles overlapping memory, such as memmove, but only if the data being copied is not a string (since memmove does not guarantee null-termination). If you must copy strings and overlap is possible, first copy the source to a temporary buffer, then copy from the temporary buffer to the destination. Also, always ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated after copying to prevent string-related vulnerabilities.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\n// Allocate a temporary buffer to avoid overlap\nchar temp[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH];\nmemcpy(temp, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\nstrncpy(info->version, temp, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\ninfo->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you can guarantee that the source and destination do not overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and add runtime assertions if possible.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
