            {
              "id": "0f050bab-d8f8-3792-9fa3-e3c6dba22c10",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's size is unknown and the length argument used in the copy operation is not proven to be within the bounds of the destination buffer. The use of a character pointer as the destination, without any evidence that the length argument is derived from the actual buffer size, increases the risk of a buffer overflow. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe, but this does not mitigate the risk of overflowing the destination buffer. There are no guards in place to ensure the length is within safe bounds, and the analysis did not detect any overlap or null pointer dereference risks. Overall, the lack of capacity checks and absence of null-termination make this a high-risk issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate this issue, ensure that the length argument used in the copy operation does not exceed the actual size of the destination buffer. If the size of the destination buffer is known, use it directly or calculate the minimum between the buffer size and the source string length plus one for the null terminator. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\n// Example fix assuming 'name' has a known size, e.g., NAME_SIZE\nsize_t name_capacity = NAME_SIZE;\nstrncpy(name, socket->get_socket_id().c_str(), name_capacity - 1);\nname[name_capacity - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf the size of the destination buffer is not known, consider refactoring the code to use safer string handling functions or C++ string objects that manage memory automatically.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2848d831-a937-3fec-bcab-5526797940b7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The code copies data from one structure to another using a standard memory copy function, but dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions. This can result in unpredictable behavior, as the standard memory copy function does not guarantee correct results when source and destination overlap. Additionally, the analysis could not rule out the possibility that the source pointer may be null, which would also cause undefined behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence, actionable problem.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use a memory move function that is safe for overlapping memory, such as `std::memmove`, instead of `std::memcpy`. This ensures that the copy operation works correctly even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(&header_value, &tmp_header_info, sizeof(metrics_table_header_t));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both source and destination pointers are valid and not null before performing the operation. If there is any chance that the source pointer could be null, add a check before the copy:\n\n```cpp\nif (&tmp_header_info != nullptr) {\n    std::memmove(&header_value, &tmp_header_info, sizeof(metrics_table_header_t));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e6c1ef5d-5a8d-36c8-ae99-94952fcaca2a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the code copies data from a C-style string into a character buffer using a length argument of 'len-1', but there is no check to ensure that 'len' is greater than zero. If 'len' is zero, subtracting one will underflow the value, resulting in a very large size being passed to the copy function. This can cause a buffer overflow and lead to undefined behavior or security vulnerabilities. The source is a C-style string, and the destination is a character pointer, but there is no evidence that the destination buffer's size is being checked or that the length is derived from the buffer's capacity. There are also no explicit null-terminations or guards present. All these factors indicate a real risk of buffer overflow due to missing input validation.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the buffer, ensure that the length argument is greater than zero to prevent underflow. Add a check to verify that the length is positive and, ideally, that it does not exceed the actual capacity of the destination buffer. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (len > 0) {\n    strncpy(name, vendor_name.c_str(), len - 1);\n    name[len - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nIf the actual size of the destination buffer is known, use it directly instead of relying on an external 'len' variable. Always ensure the buffer is null-terminated after copying.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3baba8f8-759a-3970-a5ba-39e8570654b0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk due to the destination buffer's capacity being unknown and the count argument not being proven safe. The destination buffer is a character pointer, and there is no evidence that its size is at least as large as the maximum string length minus one. The code copies data from a C-style string into this buffer using a potentially unsafe length, which could lead to a buffer overflow if the buffer is smaller than expected. There are no explicit checks or guards to ensure the destination buffer is large enough, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy. While the source is a C-style string and not null, and there is no risk of overlapping memory regions, the lack of capacity validation for the destination buffer is a significant concern.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data into the destination buffer, ensure that the buffer is at least as large as the maximum string length used in the copy operation. If the buffer size cannot be guaranteed, allocate a buffer of sufficient size or add a runtime check. Additionally, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\n// Example fix: Ensure buffer is large enough and explicitly null-terminate\nif (market_name != nullptr) {\n    // If possible, validate or allocate buffer of size AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH\n    std::strncpy(market_name, empty.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    market_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nIf the buffer size cannot be validated, consider using safer string handling functions or dynamic allocation to avoid overflows.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "24db3132-e3c0-3d5e-8dcb-464f13f790a4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the function `std::localtime` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in a multi-threaded context. Instead, use their thread-safe counterparts. In this case, `std::localtime` should be replaced with `std::localtime_s` (on Windows) or `localtime_r` (on POSIX systems).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\nstd::time_t timer;\nstd::tm bt = {};\n\n#if defined(_WIN32) || defined(_WIN64)\n    localtime_s(&bt, &timer);\n#else\n    localtime_r(&timer, &bt);\n#endif\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- `<ctime>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "31bd3edb-d331-3f1d-a44d-ef649b2d147e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. The risk is further supported by dataflow analysis indicating that the destination may be derived from the source. There are no indications that the source or destination pointers are null at this point, and the function explicitly assigns a null terminator after the copy, which is good practice but does not mitigate the undefined behavior risk from overlapping memory. The destination is a pointer type with no statically known capacity, and the count argument is a macro minus one, which does not provide additional safety. No guard is present to prevent underflow or overlap. Therefore, this is a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying strings where the source and destination may overlap, ensure that the memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a function that is safe for overlapping memory, such as `memmove`, or refactor the code to guarantee non-overlapping regions. For example, replace the string copy operation with:\n\n```cpp\nif (market_name != name) {\n    std::strncpy(market_name, name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    market_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, if overlap cannot be ruled out, use `memmove`:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strnlen(name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\nmemmove(market_name, name, len);\nmarket_name[len] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "04820cb6-9f4e-3df5-9ad7-05ae2dd8b59d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other security issues. These functions are often part of the C Standard Library and are included for backward compatibility, but their use is discouraged in modern programming.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is identified as a vulnerability sink. However, without more context, it's hard to provide a specific mitigation strategy. This function seems to be a custom function, not a standard C or C++ function. If this function is using prohibited C functions internally, it could lead to potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the unsafe functions with safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other safety measures. If the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is using unsafe functions internally, you should revise its implementation to use safer alternatives.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout knowing the internal implementation of `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)`, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if it's using unsafe C functions, you should replace them with safer alternatives. For example, if it's using `strcpy`, you could replace it with `strncpy` and make sure to null-terminate the string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to determine the specific library dependencies required by the code example. However, if `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is a function from a specific library, that library would be a dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible at all times."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
