                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions within a C++ program. These functions are often prohibited because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary validation, which can lead to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other serious security vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, there is no direct use of C library functions, hence no \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability is present. However, if the functions `goamdsmi_Init` or `apuInitCompleted` are using prohibited C functions internally, then the vulnerability could exist indirectly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. If you must use a C function, ensure that you perform proper input validation and bounds checking to prevent buffer overflows and other related vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the full context of the code, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if `goamdsmi_Init` or `apuInitCompleted` are using unsafe C functions, they should be rewritten to use safer alternatives.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout the full context of the code, it's hard to determine the specific library dependencies. However, if `goamdsmi_Init` or `apuInitCompleted` are part of a specific library, that library would be a dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
