                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions in the code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflow attacks. The use of such functions can lead to serious security issues such as unauthorized access, data corruption, and denial of service.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, there is no explicit use of a prohibited C function. However, if the variables `GOAMDSMI_CPU_INIT`, `goamdsmi_Init`, or `apuInitCompleted` are influenced by user input or external sources without proper validation and sanitization, it could lead to potential vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate such vulnerabilities, it is recommended to:\n\n- Avoid using unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library.\n- Always validate and sanitize all user inputs and external data.\n- Use secure coding practices and follow the principle of least privilege.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the full context of the code, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if the variables are influenced by user input or external data, they should be properly validated and sanitized. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (isValid(GOAMDSMI_CPU_INIT) && isValid(goamdsmi_Init) && isValid(apuInitCompleted)) {\n    (GOAMDSMI_CPU_INIT == goamdsmi_Init) || apuInitCompleted\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `isValid()` is a hypothetical function that validates the input.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet does not appear to require any specific library dependencies. However, the full context of the code might require certain libraries.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may require a subscription or membership to access certain resources."
