                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe. This is because these functions do not perform bounds checking and other necessary safety measures, which can lead to buffer overflow attacks, memory corruption, and other serious security issues. \n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_3)` is considered as a vulnerability sink. However, without more context, it's hard to provide a specific mitigation advice or source code fix recommendation. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nGenerally, to mitigate this type of vulnerability, you should avoid using unsafe C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform necessary safety checks. For example, instead of using `strcpy()`, use `strncpy()` which allows you to specify the maximum number of characters to be copied, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAs mentioned earlier, without more context, it's hard to provide a specific source code fix recommendation for the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_3)`. However, if this function is using any unsafe C functions, you should replace them with their safer alternatives.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to list all library dependencies required by the code example to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and working for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
